{"input_text": "Question: How do we calculate the limit
 \begin{align*}
\lim_{x\to 0}\left(e^{x} + 2x\right)^{3/x}
\end{align*} 
using L'Hôpital?
I don't understand how to differentiate and evaluate the limit. 
 Could anyone help me on this? 

Answer: Solution: 
 \begin{align}
&\underset{x\rightarrow 0}{\lim}~(e^x+2x)^{3/x}\\
=&\exp\left[\underset{x\rightarrow 0}{\lim}~\frac{3}{x}\ln(e^x+2x)\right]\\
=&\exp\left[3\underset{x\rightarrow 0}{\lim}~\frac{\ln(e^x+2x)}{x}\right]\\
=&\exp\left[3\underset{x\rightarrow 0}{\lim}~\left(\frac{e^x+2}{e^x+2x}\right)/1\right]~~~(\text{L'Hôpital's rule})\\
