{"input_text": "Question: This question may be a little silly, but in a recent test I was asked to evaluate this integral, which I now know how to calculate with a contour integral, but doing the residues for two double poles seems absolutely insane in such short time, as the derivatives look horrible, so I was wondering if I am missing some faster method. 
 $$\int\limits_{-\infty}^{\infty} \frac{\cos (4 x)}{\left(x^4+5 x ^ 2+4\right)^2}\,\mathrm dx$$ 

Answer: First, it is easy to get
 \begin{eqnarray}
\int_{-\infty}^\infty\frac{\cos(4x)}{x^2+a}dx=\frac{e^{-4\sqrt a}}{\sqrt a}
\end{eqnarray} 
by a contour integral. So
 \begin{eqnarray}
\int_{-\infty}^\infty\frac{\cos(4x)}{(x^2+a)(x^2+b)}dx=\frac1{b-a}\bigg(\frac{e^{-4\sqrt b}}{\sqrt b}-\frac{e^{-4\sqrt a}}{\sqrt a}\bigg)
\end{eqnarray} 
and hence
 \begin{eqnarray}
\int_{-\infty}^\infty\frac{\cos(4x)}{(x^2+1)^2(x^2+4)^2}dx=\frac{\partial^2}{\partial a\partial b}\frac1{b-a}\bigg(\frac{e^{-4\sqrt b}}{\sqrt b}-\frac{e^{-4\sqrt a}}{\sqrt a}\bigg)\bigg|_{a=1,b=4}&=&\frac{43+88e^4}{432e^8}\pi.
\end{eqnarray} 
