Answer: Use the wedge product instead of the cross product.  https://www.av8n.com/physics/clifford-intro.htm 
 First let  $u_1=x_1-x_3$ , and  $u_2=x_2-x_3$ , and  $v_1=y_1-y_3$ , and  $v_2=y_2-y_3$ , to simplify the notation. 
 $$X=u_1\wedge u_2$$ 
 $$Y=v_1\wedge v_2$$ 
 The bivector  $X$  represents the 2D plane containing the three points  $x_1,x_2,x_3$ . Similarly for  $Y$ . 
 Take the  inner product  of the two bivectors: 
 $$X^\sim=u_2\wedge u_1$$ 
 $$X^\sim\bullet Y=(u_2\wedge u_1)\bullet(v_1\wedge v_2)$$ 
 $$=(u_1\cdot v_1)(u_2\cdot v_2)-(u_1\cdot v_2)(u_2\cdot v_1)$$ 
